                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It took me a lot of tries to get into this game.\n\nThis was my first game purchase back in February 2026, and I've only just given it a proper chance now. Back then, I was obsessed with Stardew Valley-like games, which was the main reason I bought this during a sale.\n\nThe reason I didn't enjoy it at first was because I kept comparing it to Stardew Valley. Now, however, I've realized that this game has its own unique charm. It provided the variety I needed once I started moving away from my obsession with SDV-style gameplay. Progression is admittedly quite slow, especially in the prologue, but once I became familiar with the mechanics and gameplay loop, the hours just flew by. I can easily see myself sinking a lot more time into this game.\n\nI don't always understand some of the humor, largely because I'm Asian and the game is heavily rooted in English-speaking culture. That said, I still appreciate the amount of care and effort that went into its art, design, music, and world-building. The proverbs and books scattered throughout the world add so much character and depth to the game; it made me less reliant to the wiki and guides.\n\nIt's the game of the genre that I have played that made me feel like I didn't have to rush or optimize everything. Unlike Stardew Valley and Graveyard Keeper, I never felt frustrated enough to rage quit. (I might haven't progressed enough yet to do so)\n\nIronically, this feeling exists despite the sheer number of things you can do in the game. After all, it is a multi-generational life sim. Some people argue that the generational system is unnecessary because it's possible to accomplish everything in a single lifetime, and maybe they're right. But perhaps Kynseed is simply a game that encourages you to slow down, take your time, enjoy doing whatever feels fun in the moment, and trade your life with quirky trinkets.\n\nThis is the first time I've written a review for a game because I genuinely believe Kynseed deserves more recognition. If you're a fan of this genre, I highly recommend giving it a chance.",
